from . import T
T('5-automata','dfa','Finite automata;DFA','DFA states encode finite memory',r'''
A deterministic finite automaton has a finite state set, alphabet, one start state, accepting states and one transition for each state/symbol pair. Reading a word follows transitions left to right; accept only if the final state is accepting after all symbols are consumed. A state should have a meaning describing information remembered about the prefix.
A complete DFA includes rejecting behavior for every input transition, often through a sink. To complement a language, first ensure completeness, then swap accepting and nonaccepting states. Minimization merges states only when every future suffix gives the same acceptance outcome. Start by separating accepting from rejecting states and refine distinctions through successor blocks.
A DFA remembers only one of finitely many situations, not an unbounded exact count. It can remember parity or remainder because those have finitely many cases. Unreachable states do not affect the recognized language and are removed in minimization. The empty word follows no transitions, so its acceptance depends entirely on whether the start state is accepting.
''','Construct a DFA over {0,1} for an odd number of ones.',r'State meanings::::E means even count; O means odd count. Start in E.~Transitions::::Zero preserves state; one toggles E↔O.~Acceptance::::Make only O accepting; 101 has two ones and finishes E.', 'Two states suffice; 101 is rejected and 111 accepted.','The empty word has zero ones and is rejected because the start state is nonaccepting.')
T('5-automata','nfa','NFA','NFA branches and subset construction',r'''
An NFA can have several next states for one symbol and may have epsilon transitions consuming no input. It accepts when at least one complete computation consumes the whole word and ends in an accepting state; other rejecting branches do not invalidate that success. Epsilon closure collects states reachable by zero or more epsilon moves, including the starting states themselves.
Subset construction produces a DFA whose state is the set of NFA states possible after a prefix. Start with epsilon closure of the NFA start. For each symbol, union all corresponding transitions and take epsilon closure again. A subset is accepting if it contains any NFA accepting state. The empty subset can be a rejecting sink.
With n NFA states there are at most 2ⁿ subsets, but only reachable subsets need appear, and minimization can reduce them further. NFA nondeterminism adds succinctness, not language-recognition power: DFA and NFA recognize exactly regular languages. In traces, do not choose just one branch unless proving acceptance by a witness; rejecting a word requires ruling out all accepting branches.
''','NFA start q0 loops on 0 and on 1 goes to both q0 and final q1; q1 has no moves.',r'Initial subset::S_0=\{q0\}::No epsilon moves are specified.~Read first 1::S_1=\{q0,q1\}::Both nondeterministic branches are retained, so the prefix is accepted.~Read next 0::S_2=\{q0\}::q1 contributes no transition; the branch in q0 continues.', 'Word 1 is accepted; word 10 is rejected.','An accepting intermediate state is insufficient if remaining input cannot be consumed on that branch.')
T('5-automata','regex','Regular expressions','Regular expressions: union, concatenation and star',r'''
Regular expressions describe languages using union, concatenation and Kleene star. Union allows words from either operand, concatenation joins a word from the first with a word from the second, and star permits zero or more concatenated repetitions. Star therefore always includes the empty word. The empty language and the language containing only epsilon are different.
Parentheses and precedence matter: star binds to its operand, concatenation usually binds before union. A character alphabet symbol denotes a one-character word, while an expression represents a set of words. Translating to an NFA uses branches for union, joining for concatenation and looping with an empty bypass for star.
Regular expressions and finite automata have equal expressive power. A regex can constrain finite suffixes or modular counts but cannot match arbitrary nested parentheses in the classical model. Programming-language regex libraries may add nonregular features such as backreferences, so use the mathematical definition in theory questions. Prove membership by decomposing the candidate word according to the expression, not by comparing only its length.
''','Describe (0|1)*01 and test 01,101,010.',r'Prefix language::::The star permits any binary prefix, including empty.~Fixed suffix::::Concatenation requires a final zero then one.~Test words::::01 and 101 end in 01; 010 ends in 10.', 'Exactly binary words ending in 01; the first two candidates are accepted, the last rejected.','An empty starred prefix is legal, so 01 itself must not be excluded.')
T('5-regular-pumping','closure','Regular languages;Closure properties','Regular-language closure and product automata',r'''
Regular languages are those recognized by finite automata. They are closed under union, intersection, complement, difference, concatenation and star. Closure means an operation on regular operands produces another regular language; it does not mean an arbitrary operand becomes regular merely by intersecting with something.
A product DFA tracks a pair of component states. On each symbol update both coordinates. For intersection accept when both components accept; for union when either accepts; for difference when the first accepts and the second does not. Complement requires a complete deterministic machine. Concatenation and star are often more naturally constructed with epsilon NFAs.
Closure also supplies nonregularity proofs: assuming L regular and applying closed operations with known regular languages can produce a known contradiction. State which operands are regular and which closure rule is used. A finite language is regular because its finitely many words can be encoded by a finite machine. An infinite language can also be regular; infinity alone says nothing about unbounded memory requirements.
''','Recognize binary words with even zeros and even ones.',r'Component states::::Each parity machine has E,O, updating only for its counted symbol.~Product::Q=\{(E,E),(E,O),(O,E),(O,O)\}::Track both finite facts simultaneously.~Acceptance::::Start at (E,E) and accept only that pair; 0011 returns there.', 'A four-state product DFA recognizes the intersection.','The empty word has even counts of both symbols and is accepted.')
T('5-regular-pumping','pumping','Regular pumping lemma','Regular pumping lemma and the proof game',r'''
If a language is regular, some pumping length p ensures every sufficiently long accepted word has a decomposition xyz with |xy|≤p, |y|>0, and xyⁱz in the language for all i≥0. A repeated DFA state along the early prefix supplies the nonempty loop y; skipping or repeating it preserves the ending state.
To prove nonregularity, assume regularity, let p be arbitrary, then choose a word depending on p. The decomposition is not your choice: prove that every legal split fails for some exponent you can select. The chosen exponent may depend on the split. A single bad split does not contradict a condition requiring existence of one good split.
The lemma is necessary, not a general sufficient criterion for regularity. Passing some pumping examples is no proof; build a finite automaton or regex for that purpose. The word must belong to the language and have length at least p. Keep all three constraints explicit, since moving y outside the early prefix or allowing empty y destroys common counting-language arguments.
''','Prove L={aⁿbⁿ:n≥0} nonregular.',r'Choose word::w=a^pb^p::It belongs to L and is long enough.~Cover all splits::|xy|\le p,\ |y|>0\Rightarrow y=a^k,\ k\ge1::Every legal pumping part lies entirely in the a block.~Pump down::xy^0z=a^{p-k}b^p\notin L::Counts differ for every possible k.', 'L is not regular.','The reasoning defeats every legal decomposition, not merely one selected convenient split.')
T('5-cfg-pda','grammar','Context-free grammars;Context-free languages;Ambiguity','CFG derivations, parse trees and ambiguity',r'''
A context-free grammar rewrites one nonterminal at a time using productions A→α, independent of surrounding context. Starting from the start symbol, a derivation produces terminal words. The generated language contains all terminal strings attainable, not intermediate forms with nonterminals. Recursive productions can enforce unbounded balanced structure.
A parse tree records hierarchical production choices. A grammar is ambiguous if one terminal string has two distinct parse trees, equivalently two distinct leftmost derivations. Merely expanding independent branches in different orders can give the same tree and is not enough. Expression grammars often become unambiguous by assigning separate levels for precedence and recursion for associativity.
A CFL is a language with some CFG; ambiguity is a property of a chosen grammar, except when the language is inherently ambiguous across all its grammars. A grammar generating a regular language can still be written recursively, so its appearance does not automatically establish nonregularity. Verify generated strings for ordering as well as counts and include epsilon if the productions allow it.
''','Why E→E+E | E*E | id is ambiguous for id+id*id?',r'One tree::::Choose + at the root, giving id+(id*id).~Second tree::::Choose * at the root, giving (id+id)*id.~Compare::::Both yield the same terminal word but different operator grouping.', 'This grammar is ambiguous.','Two distinct structural trees, rather than two arbitrary expansion orders, establish the required witness.')
T('5-cfg-pda','pda','Push-down automata','Pushdown automata and stack-based matching',r'''
A pushdown automaton augments finite control with an unbounded stack. A transition can inspect input or epsilon, read the stack top and replace it with a string. The stack supports last-in-first-out matching obligations, allowing balanced nesting and equal ordered blocks that finite automata cannot generally count.
For aⁿbⁿ, push one marker per a, switch to the b phase and pop one per b. Reject an unmatched b, an a after the phase switch, or leftover markers at completion. A bottom marker distinguishes an empty count from losing the stack’s structural base. Acceptance by final state and by empty stack are equivalent language models for nondeterministic PDAs under suitable constructions, but a given machine’s acceptance condition must be followed exactly.
Nondeterministic PDAs recognize exactly CFLs, while deterministic PDAs recognize a proper subclass. One stack does not grant the full power of a Turing tape. In GATE traces, record state, remaining input and stack content at each step; stack orientation must be stated so push/pop order is unambiguous.
''','Trace a PDA for aⁿbⁿ on aabb using marker X and bottom Z.',r'Push a block::::After two a symbols, stack is XXZ with top at the left.~Pop b block::::The first b removes one X; the second removes the other.~Completion::::Input is consumed and only Z remains, satisfying the chosen acceptance condition.', 'aabb is accepted.','aab leaves a marker and is rejected; aabb a violates the phase order even if some count condition later changes.')
T('5-cfl-pumping','cfl-proof','Context-free pumping lemma;Non context-free languages','CFL pumping and closure limits',r'''
The CFL pumping lemma decomposes a sufficiently long word as uvxyz, with |vxy|≤p and |vy|>0, and requires uvⁱxyⁱz in the language for every nonnegative i. Two separated pieces are pumped together because a repeated nonterminal in a parse-tree path surrounds an inner subtree. Either v or y can be empty, but not both.
For non-context-freeness, choose a word after p, cover every legal bounded window and show a pumping exponent breaks membership. A three-block equal-count language is a common example: a short window cannot affect all three widely separated counts. Boundary-straddling windows must be handled too, not just windows inside one block.
CFLs are closed under union, concatenation and star, and intersection with a regular language. They are not generally closed under complement or intersection with another CFL. A closure argument must use a valid operand class. As with regular pumping, the lemma is a necessary condition rather than a universal recipe for proving that a language is context-free; a grammar or PDA gives that positive proof.
''','Show why aᵖbᵖcᵖ is useful against aⁿbⁿcⁿ.',r'Window bound::|vxy|\le p::A window touching a and c would need all p middle b symbols plus endpoints and be too long.~Nonempty pumping::|vy|>0::Removing v,y changes at least one block count.~Untouched block::::At least one original p-count stays unchanged, so equal counts fail, or ordering is disrupted.', 'The witness defeats every legal split and proves the language is not context-free.','Both pieces need not be nonempty; the argument uses their combined positive length.')
T('5-decidability','machines','Turing machines','Turing machines, recognizers and deciders',r'''
A Turing machine has finite control and an unbounded tape whose head reads, writes and moves according to transition rules. Tape memory can be revisited and changed, unlike the restricted top access of a single PDA stack. A machine computation may halt accepting, halt rejecting or continue forever.
A recognizer for L must eventually accept every member; on nonmembers it may reject or loop. A decider must halt on every input with the correct yes/no result. Decidable languages are recognizable, but recognizable languages need not be decidable. Flipping accept/reject outcomes complements a decider, yet cannot repair a recognizer’s infinite loops.
Equivalent common machine variants describe the same computable languages despite efficiency differences. To prove decidability, provide an algorithm and a termination argument, not just plausible accepting behavior. If both L and its complement are recognizable, dovetail both recognizers; whichever accepts first determines the answer, and one must eventually accept. Simulation needs saved configurations so neither branch monopolizes execution.
''','Given recognizers R for L and S for its complement, decide membership.',r'Interleave::::Run one step of R, then one of S, repeatedly on the same input.~Positive case::::If R accepts, answer yes; do not require S to halt.~Negative case::::If S accepts, answer no. Every input belongs to one side, guaranteeing eventual acceptance there.', 'Dovetailing constructs a decider.','Running R to completion before S could loop forever on a nonmember and would not be a decider.')
T('5-decidability','reductions','Undecidability;Halting problem;Reductions','Undecidability and reduction direction',r'''
An undecidable decision problem has no algorithm halting with the correct answer on every permitted input. This does not mean every instance is unknowable or that no recognizer exists. The halting problem asks whether a supplied machine halts on a supplied input; a universal decider would contradict a self-referential diagonal construction.
A mapping reduction A≤ₘB transforms each input x of A into f(x) for B by an always-halting computable function, preserving x∈A iff f(x)∈B. If B were decidable, computing f then using its decider would decide A. Thus to prove B undecidable, reduce a known undecidable A to B, not in the reverse direction.
The transform cannot hide an undecidable step of its own. State the constructed machine and the behavior in both source yes/no cases. Rice’s theorem concerns nontrivial semantic properties of recognized languages, not every syntactic fact about a machine description. A finite bound on simulation time decides bounded halting, a different question from unrestricted eventual halting.
''','A known undecidable A reduces to B. What follows if B has a decider?',r'Transform input::x\mapsto f(x)::The reduction always halts.~Use assumed solver::::Call B’s decider on f(x), which also always halts.~Preserve answer::x\in A\iff f(x)\in B::Return that answer to decide A, contradicting its undecidability.', 'B cannot have a decider.','A reduction B≤A would not establish this conclusion, because B might be an easier problem.')
T('6-lexical','tokens','Lexical analysis;Tokens;Longest match','Tokenization, maximal munch and priority',r'''
Lexical analysis turns source characters into token occurrences. A token kind names a category such as identifier, number or operator; its lexeme is the actual source substring and an attribute can store a value or symbol-table reference. Whitespace and comments can be skipped while position tracking remains. The parser uses these tokens to determine grammatical structure.
Maximal munch selects the longest prefix matching any token rule. If several rules match the same maximum length, a priority convention selects among them. A lexer can scan forward with a DFA while remembering its last accepting position, then return there when no longer extension is possible. Keyword priority therefore does not split ifx into if and x when the whole word matches an identifier.
Regular expressions describe typical token classes; nested syntax and arbitrary balanced dependencies belong to parsing rather than a finite lexer rule. Count token occurrences, not distinct categories or characters. For GATE traces explicitly note the equal-length tie rule and whether punctuation, comments and whitespace emit tokens.
''','Rules include > and >=. Tokenize a>=10.',r'Identifier::::The first maximal token is a.~Operator::::Both > and >= begin here, but >= consumes more characters and wins.~Number::::The remaining digits form one numeric token 10.', 'Three tokens: identifier(a), ≥ operator, number(10).','Rule priority matters only if maximum lengths tie; placing > first does not beat the longer >= match.')
T('6-parsing','first-follow','Parsing;FIRST;FOLLOW;LL','Predictive parsing, nullable prefixes and tables',r'''
Parsing checks whether a token sequence follows a grammar and builds its structure. FIRST(α) contains terminals beginning strings derived from α, plus epsilon if α can vanish. FOLLOW(A) contains terminals that can appear immediately after nonterminal A in a sentential form; the end marker follows the start symbol. Compute nullable, FIRST and FOLLOW repeatedly until stable because indirect dependencies can propagate over several passes.
An LL(1) parser uses one lookahead terminal to choose a production. Place A→α under each non-epsilon terminal in FIRST(α). If α is nullable, also place it under FOLLOW(A). Two demanded productions in one cell constitute a conflict. A terminal on the stack must match the lookahead; a nonterminal selects a table action.
Nullable prefixes require looking further right when calculating FIRST. Left recursion prevents ordinary predictive descent, while left factoring can postpone a shared-prefix choice; neither transformation by itself guarantees LL(1). An unambiguous grammar can still fail LL(1). Explain why each table entry is chosen instead of memorizing a set without its dependency source.
''','S→Ab, A→a|ε. Find FIRST(S), FOLLOW(A) and A’s table entries.',r'FIRST::FIRST(S)=\{a,b\}::A either starts with a or vanishes, exposing b.~FOLLOW::FOLLOW(A)=\{b\}::The terminal after A is mandatory b.~Predictive choices::::Use A→a on a and A→ε on b; cells do not conflict.', 'FIRST(S)={a,b}; FOLLOW(A)={b}; two distinct A entries.','Epsilon is absent from FIRST(S) because the trailing b cannot vanish.')
T('6-parsing','lr','LR','LR items, closure/goto and reduction conflicts',r'''
An LR parser reads input left-to-right and builds a rightmost derivation in reverse using shifts and reductions. An item A→α·β marks how much of a production has been recognized. Closure adds items for a nonterminal immediately after a dot; goto advances dots over one grammar symbol and closes the resulting set. These item sets form parser states.
A shift consumes a terminal and advances the state stack. A reduction pops the production’s right-hand-side extent and uses goto on its left nonterminal. LR(0) completed items propose reduction without lookahead restriction; SLR uses FOLLOW sets; LR(1) carries item-specific lookahead, and LALR merges suitable cores. The choice affects which grammars avoid conflicts.
A shift/reduce conflict offers both actions in one state/lookahead cell; reduce/reduce offers two reductions. Ambiguity is one possible source but parser-family limitations can also create conflicts for unambiguous grammars. Do not use all lookaheads of one completed item for every other item. Trace both grammar symbols and parser states, since a reduction changes their stack roles differently.
''','Item E→E·+T sees lookahead +. Is the expression E already ready for this production’s reduction?',r'Read dot position::::The +T suffix is still unrecognized in this item.~Advance on plus::E\to E+\cdot T::A transition over + shifts input and moves the dot.~Completion condition::E\to E+T\cdot::Only after T is recognized is this production complete.', 'This item suggests shifting +, not reducing E→E+T yet.','A completed item’s dot is at the far right; it is a different item from the one initially supplied.')
T('6-translation-runtime','attributes','Syntax-directed translation;Attributes','Attribute dependencies and syntax-directed evaluation',r'''
Syntax-directed translation attaches semantic rules to grammar productions, computing values such as expression results, types or generated code. Synthesized attributes flow from child computations to a parent. Inherited attributes obtain information from the parent or allowed siblings, such as a declaration’s type flowing into several identifiers.
A dependency graph connects the attribute values required to compute others. A legal evaluation order must be topological; a cycle prevents straightforward evaluation for that tree. S-attributed definitions use only synthesized attributes and fit bottom-up evaluation. L-attributed restrictions allow a left-to-right depth-first evaluation with certain inherited dependencies, not arbitrary dependencies on future siblings.
The parse tree determines grouping, and semantic evaluation must follow its dependencies rather than the unparenthesized character order. Some rules emit effects, so execution order matters in translation schemes. A GATE question asking whether a definition is S- or L-attributed needs inspection of every dependency, not just noticing one inherited attribute or one bottom-up-looking production.
''','Evaluate a parse of 2+3*4 with multiplication below the addition node.',r'Leaf values::::Literal nodes supply 2,3,4.~Child computation::3\cdot4=12::The multiplication attribute is available before its parent uses it.~Parent computation::2+12=14::Synthesize the root value from child results.', 'Root value 14.','A different tree grouping (2+3)*4 would produce 20; semantics depend on the specified structure.')
T('6-translation-runtime','frames','Runtime environments;Activation records','Activation records, static links and dynamic links',r'''
A runtime environment manages storage and control during execution. Each live function call normally has an activation record containing arguments, locals, saved state and return information under the calling convention. Recursive calls need distinct simultaneous records, even though they execute the same source procedure.
The dynamic link references the caller’s activation and supports returning through the call chain. The static link references the lexically enclosing environment for access to nonlocal variables in nested procedures. Caller and lexical parent can differ, so those links are not interchangeable. A display is an alternative way to locate enclosing environments.
Storage allocation depends on lifetime: static-duration data can have a fixed location, stack allocation follows properly nested calls, and heap allocation supports lifetimes extending beyond a call. Returning releases the current frame and resumes the saved caller, not a fresh invocation. In GATE traces keep source-level names separate from their per-activation storage instances and identify whether nesting is lexical or merely a call relation.
''','Calls main→f→f occur recursively, and each f has local x.',r'Create first frame::::The first f receives its own x object and return address to main.~Recurse::::The second f gets a different x object and return address into the first f.~Return::::Destroy the inner frame and resume the outer f with its saved x.', 'The two f activations have distinct locals.','One source declaration can correspond to several simultaneously live runtime objects.')
T('6-ir-dataflow','ir','Intermediate code generation;Local optimisation','Three-address code and safe local optimization',r'''
Intermediate code exposes computations in a machine-independent form, such as three-address instructions assigning one operation’s result to a temporary. Lower an expression following precedence and parse dependencies; labels and branches encode control flow. A basic block has a single entry and no internal control transfer except its terminating instruction, letting local optimization reason within that region.
Constant folding evaluates operations with known literal values; copy propagation substitutes known copies; dead-code elimination removes results that are not used, subject to observable effects. Algebraic rewrites must obey language semantics: signed overflow, floating rounding, volatile accesses and function effects can invalidate apparently familiar identities.
A DAG for a block can share repeated pure computations, but operand redefinition or aliasing writes can make a later expression different. Optimization correctness means preserving permitted observable behavior, not merely generating fewer lines. Distinguish a temporary with one definition from a source variable reassigned several times, and preserve dependencies when choosing instruction order.
''','Translate a=b+c*d into three-address code.',r'Product::::Emit t1=c*d, because multiplication supplies an operand to addition.~Addition::::Emit t2=b+t1 after t1 is defined.~Assignment::::Emit a=t2; the source destination gets the whole expression result.', 't1=c*d; t2=b+t1; a=t2.','Substituting temporary definitions reconstructs b+c*d with original precedence.')
T('6-ir-dataflow','constants','Constant propagation','Constant propagation over control-flow merges',r'''
Constant propagation determines whether a variable has one known value at a program point. A straight-line assignment x=3 makes subsequent safe uses replaceable by 3 until a possible reassignment invalidates that fact. Folding then computes operations whose operands are constants. These are related but different transformations.
At a control-flow merge, a value is a constant only when all reachable incoming paths agree on it. Different constants become unknown/nonconstant, not their average. Unreachable paths and “not yet known” information need a lattice convention distinct from a definitely varying value. Transfer functions process assignments and kills; loops require iteration to a fixed point.
Calls and aliasing memory writes can invalidate values unless analysis proves they cannot change the object. A compiler cannot choose the value from the branch it happens to inspect first. GATE questions usually simplify this model, but still require following every predecessor. State whether analysis is flow-sensitive and whether the question gives a single block or a graph with joins.
''','One branch sets x=3, another x=3, then y=x+2. Change the second branch to x=4.',r'Equal branches::::With 3 on both paths, x is known as 3 at the merge.~Fold::y=3+2=5::Propagation and folding are safe in that first program.~Different branches::::With 3 and 4, x is not a single constant, so y cannot be replaced globally by 5.', 'First program folds y to 5; the changed program does not.','The merge must represent both reachable paths rather than selecting one predecessor’s value.')
T('6-ir-dataflow','liveness','Liveness analysis','Live variables and backward fixed-point equations',r'''
A variable’s current value is live if some future path reads it before overwriting it. This is about demand for the present value, not whether the variable exists or has been initialized. USE[B] includes reads needing incoming values; DEF[B] records definitions in a block. Moving backward, definitions kill old-value demand and uses add demand.
At block exit collect the union of live-in sets from successors, since use on any path matters. Then IN[B]=USE[B]∪(OUT[B]−DEF[B]). Iterate across loops until sets stop changing, beginning from the appropriate boundary conditions. Union is chosen because liveness is a may-property; an all-path available-expression analysis uses intersection instead.
Liveness supports register allocation and dead-assignment removal, but deleting a statement also requires preserving side effects. In a block like x=x+1, x is both used and defined, and its incoming value is live because the read precedes the write. Analyze statement order when computing USE, not simply every variable appearing anywhere.
''','USE={a}, DEF={b}, OUT={b,c}. Find IN.',r'Remove overwritten value::OUT-DEF=\{c\}::Old b is not required just because new b is live later.~Add direct use::USE\cup\{c\}=\{a,c\}::a’s incoming value is read within the block.~Interpret::::The entering values of a and c are needed, while old b is not.', 'IN={a,c}.','The result concerns old incoming values; b can still have a live newly assigned value on exit.')
T('6-ir-dataflow','cse','Common sub expression elimination','Available expressions and common-subexpression reuse',r'''
Common-subexpression elimination replaces a repeated computation by a previously obtained value only if its operands have not changed and the operation is safely reusable. In a straight block, an assignment to either operand invalidates availability; a potentially aliased write or impure call can do the same for memory-dependent expressions.
Across control flow, an expression is available at a point only when computed and not killed on every incoming path. Thus the merge is intersection, unlike liveness’s union. GEN records expressions established and still valid by block end; KILL records expressions invalidated by assigned operands. Iteration handles loops and shared successors.
The text a+b appearing twice is not enough: values at the two points must be equal under program semantics. Commutativity-based matching also depends on the operator and type; floating-point and effects demand care. GATE examples often include an intervening assignment precisely to distinguish visual duplication from valid availability. Removing an evaluation that can trap or has effects needs a stronger correctness argument.
''','t1=a+b; c=7; t2=a+b; then change c=7 to a=7.',r'Original operands::::Assigning c does not change a or b under the simple nonaliasing model.~Reuse::::Replace t2=a+b by t2=t1 in the original block.~Changed program::::Assigning a kills the previous a+b value; recomputation is necessary.', 'Reuse is valid in the first block and invalid after redefining a.','An expression’s spelling stayed the same, but one operand’s value changed in the second program.')
